---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SideMark from '../../components/SideMark.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Stores Designer — Andrew Shiau"
  description="A design-system tool at Amazon that helps distributed teams build consistent shopping experiences."
>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="back"><a href="/">← Index</a></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-10">
      <SideMark href="/work/stores-designer/" />
      <h1>Stores Designer</h1>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <p class="dek c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      Helping distributed teams at Amazon build consistent shopping experiences
      without hand-coding each one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="facts">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Role</div>
        <div class="ftx">Frontend engineer — design-system tooling and the design system that keeps what teams build consistent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Where</div>
        <div class="ftx">At Amazon · 2023–present.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>The premise</h2>
      <div class="sbody">
        <p>
          Many teams, one shopping surface. Left alone, each team rebuilds the same
          components slightly differently and consistency erodes. The job is to make the
          consistent path the easy path — a design system plus tooling that lets people
          build real experiences quickly, on shared rails, without hand-coding every
          screen.
        </p>
        <p>
          The principle: build against a shared system so consistency is the default,
          not a thing you police afterward.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>What I work on</h2>
      <div class="sbody">
        <p>
          Front-end for the tooling, and the design system that keeps what teams build
          consistent. The way I think about AI on this site — verification loops, keeping
          a human in the judgment seat — comes from working on problems like this one.
        </p>
        <p class="fine">
          This is production work at Amazon, so it's kept deliberately high level here —
          no internal detail.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
